  "notes": "Pocket-first, and the pocket is documented: the Splice breakdown names the jetsonmade signature as **unwavering straight 16th hats with downbeat-focused 808s**, so `hihat.base` is a single `16th` rather than trap's weighted choice, `fillDensity` is high at 0.68, and the roll frequency and open-hat probability are the lowest half-time figures in this batch. The 808 matches: `staccato`, `slideProb` 0.12 — nine times less likely to slide than `slim-jxmmi` — anchored on the downbeat with `kick.syncopation` 0.28, because the research says it deliberately leaves the \"and\" of beats open for the vocal. \"Listen for the most simple piece\" is `monophonicBias` 0.92 and a two-part texture: chopped piano or soul sample plus one bell or key layer, which is `countermelody.densityRatio` 0.34. ⚠ `confidence` medium (research: high-medium). The INFERRED parameter is `session.bpm`: the research names two distinct humps — 140–150 on the bounce cuts and 105–110 on the disco/soul-flip singles — and a single tempo block cannot state two, so this one spans both with its mode on the bounce lane. ⚠ The sample-flip harmony is real here rather than decorative: a full chord bed with ii–v–i and I–vi–IV–V families and `seventh` at 0.4, because the research says the source dictates a major/modal 4-bar loop. ⚠ `anticipationProb` raised to 0.15. With `mirror_kick` against a fully locked 808 both parts take every kick, so every bass onset lands exactly on an 808 onset — `coherence.rs` calls that one line played twice rather than two parts locking. The lock is the style and is untouched; the anticipation is what makes them two parts.",
